@subsection Error handling

Every method that takes elisp returns errors as
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Error} D-Bus errors.  In @command{gdbus}
they print as:

@example
Error: GDBus.Error:org.cmacs.Editor1.Error: ((wrong-number-of-arguments) ...)
@end example

Programmatic clients should match on the error name to decide
whether to surface to the user or retry.  Most languages' D-Bus
bindings turn the error into a typed exception.  See
@ref{D-Bus CLI Examples} for examples in each language.

@subsection org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade

Drives AI agents.  Every argument and every return value is a string, so
a shell caller never has to think about D-Bus types; numeric arguments
are coerced on the far side.  All of them route through
@code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool}, the same entry point an agent reaches
over MCP---three surfaces, one code path.

@table @code
@item Spawn (task, agent, title, model) @result{} id
Hand a task to an agent.  All but the first argument may be empty.
@item Status (id), Result (id), Cancel (id), List ()
State and cost, what it produced, stop it, everything known.
@item Agents (), Providers (), Models (provider)
What can be spawned, and what it can be run against.
@item Send (id, message)
Queue a message.  Continues the existing conversation, so the agent
still remembers earlier turns; wakes a task that has already finished.
@item Inbox (id), Drop (id, index)
What is queued but not yet delivered, and removing one---an empty
@var{index} clears the queue.
@item Log (id, from_turn, kinds)
The transaction log: every message, reply and tool call.  Works on any
task, including ones the caller never spawned.  Both filters may be
empty.
@item Close (id)
Release the conversation's session.
@end table

Confirmation prompts are bypassed on this interface deliberately.  They
exist so an @emph{agent} cannot start paid work unattended; a D-Bus
caller is the session owner, who already has
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval} on the same bus.  It also could not work as
written: RPC evals bind @code{inhibit-interaction}, so a prompt inside a
dispatch signals rather than asking, and every @code{Spawn} would fail.

@example
busctl --user call org.cmacs.Editor1 /org/cmacs/Editor1 \
  org.cmacs.Editor1.Brigade Send ss "$ID" "now check the error paths"
@end example
